    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-330495",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a681cf28-41fa-5547-ba25-ee6a720e1a4f",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity",
      "problem": "In **ascending order**, rounded to two decimal places in radians, the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$ \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{8\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =8 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{8}{3})≈1.212\\,03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\nx≈1.212\\,03+\\pi ≈4.353\\,62\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈1.21,\\,{x}_{2}≈4.35.\n$$",
