    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-21607",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a681cf28-41fa-5547-ba25-ee6a720e1a4f",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity",
      "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=x+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{11\\pi }{6}=\\frac{55{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
